WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.269
Question seven.

00:00:01.269 --> 00:00:07.189
Step one: macro scan. It asks what the unique ticket number should be.

00:00:07.189 --> 00:00:10.573
One mark. Command words: “best used as”.

00:00:10.573 --> 00:00:12.095
Step two: denoise.

00:00:12.095 --> 00:00:14.886
Useful, in yellow: ticket number.

00:00:14.886 --> 00:00:18.777
In cyan: “unique in the house” and “identify”.

00:00:18.777 --> 00:00:23.345
Useless red herring: the cinema setting — kick it out.

00:00:23.345 --> 00:00:24.867
Step three: draft.

00:00:24.867 --> 00:00:26.812
Unique plus identifies.

00:00:26.812 --> 00:00:28.673
That is a primary key.

00:00:28.673 --> 00:00:31.295
It also links session and seat.

00:00:31.295 --> 00:00:32.733
Step four: P O E.

00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:36.539
A, remarks text: not guaranteed unique — cut.

00:00:36.539 --> 00:00:40.346
B, parity bit: only for error checking — cut.

00:00:40.346 --> 00:00:45.251
D, compression setting: nothing to do with identity — cut.

00:00:45.251 --> 00:00:53.540
Step five: sanity check and lock. A primary key must be unique and identify the record — both fit.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:55.824
So the correct answer is C.
